  "id": "PSI-23",
  "summary_plain": "This experiment burned gaseous fuels like hydrogen, methane, and ethylene injected through a spherical porous burner inside the International Space Station's combustion chamber. The goal was to study how spherical diffusion flames grow, change structure, and eventually extinguish in microgravity. Understanding these flame behaviors helps researchers improve combustion efficiency on Earth and prevents fires in spacecraft.",
  "summary_expert": "The ACME s-Flame investigation utilized the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) on the International Space Station to examine spherical diffusion flames using gaseous fuels including 100% H2, C2H4 with He or N2, CH4 with He or N2, and H2-CH4 mixtures. Tested oxygen concentrations ranged from 21 to 30 percent (mole fraction 0.21 to 0.30) using helium and nitrogen as diluents. Variables that changed between tests included the fuel type, oxygen concentration, and diluent. The documents provided describe the objectives, hardware designs, and test plans only.",
  "key_findings": [
